# agent-orchard — code owners
#
# Purpose: keep the security controls under different, stricter ownership than
# the plugin content those controls exist to police.
#
# The threat this addresses: a single pull request that adds a plugin *and*
# weakens the scanner in the same commit — so the check that would have caught
# the plugin never runs against it. Splitting ownership means the control paths
# and the content paths are reviewed as separate concerns by separate rules.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE RELYING ON THIS FILE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CODEOWNERS on its own enforces nothing. Three limits, stated plainly so nobody
# assumes protection that is not actually in place:
#
# 1. It is inert without branch protection. The repository ruleset for the
#    default branch must have "Require a pull request before merging" and
#    "Require review from Code Owners" enabled. Without those, this file is
#    advisory and merges proceed unblocked.
#
# 2. "Require review from Code Owners" means at least ONE owner per matching
#    rule — not all of them. To require every steward, the ruleset's required
#    approval count must be set to the number of stewards, listed individually
#    below rather than behind a team handle (a team is satisfied by any single
#    member). Both settings live in repository configuration, not in this file,
#    and neither is verifiable from the repository contents.
#
# 3. It cannot stop one PR from touching both a control path and a plugin path.
#    It requires both sets of owners to approve; it does not force the change
#    into two pull requests. Closing that gap needs a CI check that fails any
#    PR whose diff touches plugins/** together with scripts/** or .github/**.
#    That check is NOT yet implemented. Until it is, the separation below is
#    partial, and reviewers must enforce the split by reading the diff.
#
# UNRESOLVED HANDLES FAIL SILENTLY. GitHub ignores CODEOWNERS rules naming a
# user or team that does not exist, or that lacks write access to this
# repository. The rule stays in the file and looks like protection while
# enforcing nothing. Every handle below must be confirmed to resolve — check the
# repository's CODEOWNERS view for errors after the org and its teams exist.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TODO before the first plugin is merged
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The @agentorchard org and its teams do not exist at scaffold time, so the
# handles below are placeholders and currently enforce NOTHING. Replace
# @agentorchard/stewards on the control paths with the explicit list of
# individual steward handles, confirm every handle resolves, and enable the
# branch protection settings described above.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Default owner for anything not matched by a more specific rule below.
# Later rules take precedence over earlier ones.
*                       @agentorchard/stewards

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier 1 — security controls. All stewards.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These paths are the defence itself. A change here can silently disable the
# checks protecting every plugin in the marketplace, so they carry the strictest
# ownership in the repository and are never co-reviewed with plugin content.

# Scanners, transforms, the Deno wrapper, the egress hook, release tooling.
/scripts/               @agentorchard/stewards

# CI definitions, workflow permissions, this file. Anything under .github/ can
# alter or remove the checks in scripts/ without touching scripts/ at all —
# including this file, which is why it must own itself.
/.github/               @agentorchard/stewards

# Governance and licensing. Changing the working rules, the licence, or the
# attribution notices changes what the project promises users and what it owes
# authors. Same bar as the code that enforces it.
/CLAUDE.md              @agentorchard/stewards
/LICENSE                @agentorchard/stewards
/LICENSE-DATA           @agentorchard/stewards
/NOTICE                 @agentorchard/stewards
/docs/licensing.md      @agentorchard/stewards

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier 2 — mirrored content and registry data.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reviewed as plugin admissions, against the controls in Tier 1 — never in the
# same pull request as a change to those controls.
#
# These paths do not exist yet; the rules are declared ahead of the directories
# so that the first plugin PR cannot land before its ownership rule does.

/plugins/               @agentorchard/stewards
/registry/              @agentorchard/stewards
/marketplace.json       @agentorchard/stewards
